-Path to nearest unknown grid (``p``)
-  Unless the :ref:`Autoexplore Commands Option <autoexplore-commands-option>`
-  is on, this command does nothing.  When that option is on, you are not
-  confused, and no monsters are in view, ``p`` will determine a path to
-  the nearest (by turns) known passable grid that has an unknown neighbor
-  and start you along that path.  If there is no such path, it will try
-  to find a path to the nearest known passable grid that is next to a closed
-  door or impassable rubble and that door or rubble has an unknown neighbor.
-  :ref:`Pathfinding <pathfinding-player>` has more information about how the
-  path is calculated and what happens when following the path.
+Start exploring (``p``)
+  With the :ref:`Autoexplore Commands Option <autoexplore-commands-option>`
+  enabled and no monsters in view, ``p`` rests as needed (like ``R`` followed
+  by ``&``), then follows known routes to unexplored parts of the level.
+  You can cross glyphs of warding, pick locks, and dig through impassable
+  rubble when you have a chance to clear it. After ten failed attempts at
+  one grid, exploration skips that obstacle until you start exploring again.
+  Routes avoid damaging terrain and webs. If no safe route remains,
+  you try to disarm known traps that block further exploration.
+
+  Exploration heads for known treasure and items you would automatically
+  pick up when they are within ten extra movement turns of the nearest
+  unexplored area. It collects more distant items before finishing the
+  level if they are reachable. The usual pickup rules apply, including
+  limits such as ``=g5``. Ignored items are skipped.
+
+  Exploration stops with "Something is here." when you notice a monster,
+  staircase, trap, secret door, or other new item of interest. Several
+  discoveries on the same step give one message. Pressing ``p`` again
+  lets you pass items you have already seen. Other disturbances can
+  also stop exploration.
+  When no route remains, exploration reports whether a known obstacle
+  separates you from an unexplored area and whether any known items for
+  pickup are out of reach. Press Escape to interrupt resting or movement,
+  and ``p`` to explore again.
 
 Resting Commands
 ================
